<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">O Sacred Head, Now Wounded</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">Latin poem 12th or 13th century; German hymn first printed <a href="/years/1656">1656</a>; English translation <a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as O Haupt voll Blut und Wunden</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;O Sacred Head, Now Wounded&quot; is a Christian Passion hymn based on a long medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poem, &quot;Salve mundi salutare&quot;, whose sections address in turn the parts of Christ's body hanging on the Cross. Its last part, from which the hymn is taken, addresses Christ's head and begins &quot;Salve caput cruentatum&quot;.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poem was long attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a> but is now attributed to the medieval poet Arnulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a>, who died in 1250. The Lutheran hymn writer Paul Gerhardt translated the whole poem into German, reworking it as a more personal meditation on Christ's death, and it first appeared in Johann Crüger's hymnal Praxis pietatis melica in 1656. Of his version, the closing section, ten verses beginning O Haupt voll Blut und Wunden, became best known and is sung as a hymn in its own right.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>John Gambold, an Anglican vicar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxfordshire">Oxfordshire</a>, made the first English translation in 1752, beginning O Head so full of bruises. The American Presbyterian minister James Waddel Alexander made the version beginning O sacred head, now wounded in 1830, and it became one of the most widely used in hymnals of the 19th and 20th centuries. Catherine Winkworth published another in Lyra Germanica in 1855, and Sir Henry Williams Baker one in Hymns Ancient and Modern in 1861. In 1899 Robert Bridges translated afresh from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, and his text was taken into the Episcopal hymnals of 1940 and 1982 and the New English Hymnal of 1986.</p><p>The tune comes from Hans Leo Hassler, who wrote it around 1600 for a secular love song, Mein G'müt ist mir verwirret, printed in 1601. Johann Crüger simplified its rhythm for Gerhardt's hymn in 1656. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> set five stanzas in four different ways in his St Matthew Passion, used the hymn in the cantata BWV 159 and the tune in the Christmas Oratorio and the cantata BWV 161. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-liszt">Franz Liszt</a> arranged it in his Via crucis, Rued Langgaard wrote variations on it for string quartet, and Edmund Rubbra used it in the final chorus of his Ninth Symphony. The melody has also carried secular songs, among them Tom Glazer's protest song Because All Men Are Brothers of 1947 and Paul Simon's American Tune.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “O Sacred Head, Now Wounded” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
